# mail-archive.conf — mailboxes whose OLD mail gets gzipped on disk.
#
# Read by /usr/local/reqad/scripts/compress_old_mail, which runs nightly from
# /etc/cron.d/reqad. This file is empty of entries by default, and an empty file
# means the cron job does nothing at all: compression is opt-in per mailbox.
#
# Before adding anything here, Dovecot has to be able to READ compressed mail:
#
#     bash /usr/local/reqad/scripts/update/setup_dovecot_compress.sh
#
# Without that plugin the client is handed the raw gzip bytes as the message
# body — so the cron job checks for it and refuses to run rather than trusting
# this file.
#
# Syntax — one entry per line, "#" starts a comment:
#
#   days=365              set the default age cutoff for the lines BELOW it
#   user@domain.tld       compress that mailbox using the current default
#   user@domain.tld 90    ... or with its own cutoff, in days
#   @domain.tld           every mailbox in that domain, current default
#   @domain.tld 730       every mailbox in that domain, 2 years
#
# The cutoff is on file mtime, which for a maildir message is when it was
# delivered (or last flag-updated). 365 is the built-in default when no days=
# line has been seen.
#
# Compression is in place and reversible: the maildir filename never changes,
# and a message that is already gzipped is detected by its content and left
# alone, so this is safe to re-run and safe to widen later. To undo a mailbox,
# drop its line here and gunzip the files back over themselves — nothing else
# recorded that they were compressed.
#
# Try it read-only first:
#     /usr/local/reqad/scripts/compress_old_mail --dry-run --verbose

# days=365
# old@example.com 365
# @archive.example.com 180
